# MRI authorization and earlier-slot workflow

Use this for a scheduled musculoskeletal MRI when insurance pre-certification is pending or has delayed an earlier appointment.

1. Keep the existing MRI appointment until an earlier appointment is confirmed.
2. Ask the imaging center to add the patient to its cancellation list. Clarify acceptable locations, scanners, days, and short-notice availability.
3. When authorization is pending, ask the insurer/pre-certification department for the case status, expected decision date, and whether any documentation is missing.
4. Ask the ordering clinician’s office to verify that the order, diagnosis, office notes, functional limitations, conservative-treatment history, and PT findings/referral are correctly submitted.
5. If denied, request the written rationale and ask the ordering office about peer-to-peer review or appeal procedures; do not assume the MRI is permanently unavailable.
6. Use the PT evaluation to document objective findings and function, but do not promise that it will change authorization.

Patient-facing wording: “I have a scheduled MRI, authorization is pending, and I can come on short notice. Please add me to every appropriate cancellation list and let me know what information is needed to complete pre-certification.”
